1 Introduction
Virtual reality (VR) technologies are increasingly used in engineering and entertainment applications. As a result, many studies focus on enhancing the sensory feedback provided to users, to help them feel more immersed in a VR environment. In particular, many studies use both visual feedback and haptic feedback to increase the realism.
Head-mounted displays (HMDs) are often used to display virtual scenes. However, most commercial HMDs are closed, not see-through. Therefore, if users need to change or input some data in a fully immersive VR environment, they need to take off the closed HMD to access a physical keyboard. Thus, the sensation of immersion and realism of the VR applications will be interrupted.
In order to solve the above-mentioned problems, virtual keyboards were developed to allow users to directly input data. For example, Du and Charbon (2008) developed a virtual keyboard system based on multi-level feature matching. However, previous studies showed that keyboards without haptic feedback have higher typing error rates than keyboards with haptic feedback (Markov-Vetter et al. 2012; Chaparro et al. 2014). Haptic feedback has been proven to be able to improve work efﬁciency, work accuracy, and user pleasure (Fukumoto and Sugimura 2001; Koskinen et al. 2008; Tzafestas et al. 2008). Furthermore, haptic feedback can help with skill training in VR. For example, Choi and Lo (2014) developed a VR system with haptic device to help disabled children to acquire skills in activities of daily living.
Prior research used different kinds of actuators to create haptic feedback. For example, Wu et al. (2012) used pneumatic actuators in VR environments. Pneumatic actuators have slow impulse responses which make them

