unsuitable for realistic fast-reaction feedback. Romano and Kuchenbecker (2012) mounted two voice coil actuators on a stylus. They built a lookup table and used a linear predictive model to reproduce vibrations resulting from impacts against a surface. Other studies used microspeakers, piezo-electric actuators, and vibration motors to create high-frequency 50–200 Hz vibrations for haptic feedback (Hayward and Cruz-Hernandez 2000; Kyung et al. 2004; Minamizawa et al. 2007; Aoki et al. 2009; Hashimoto et al. 2009; Sziebig et al. 2009; Olsson et al. 2012).
Therefore, in order to improve human–computer interaction, virtual keyboards with haptic feedback offer the best overall experience (Markov-Vetter et al. 2012). Kim and Kim (2004) overlaid a virtual keyboard on a real keyboard to obtain haptic feedback from the physical keyboard. Markov-Vetter et al. (2012) overlaid a virtual keyboard on a physical surface. However, virtual keyboards overlaid on physical keyboards or physical surfaces limit the workspace for interacting with virtual objects. Users can only use VR haptic keyboards at ﬁxed locations. Even if users hold a physical keyboard or a physical surface at hands and move around, they might lack of extra pair of hands for typing task. De Pra et al. (2014) designed a virtual piano keyboard and a magnetic glove. They used Leap Motion and ultrasound to track ﬁnger positions. Haptic feedback can be provided using magnets when a virtual key was pressed.
Lylykangas et al. (2011) controlled different durations and delay times to create button-click sensations using piezo actuators. Park et al. (2011) chose different vibration parameters (amplitude, duration, carrier signal, envelope function, and actuator) to create button-click vibrations using micro-speakers. Park et al. also conducted user tests to identify vibrations that were most realistic. Prior studies provided users with different simulated haptic feedback. However, none of them created realistic haptic feedback using actual vibration patterns from physical keyboards. In addition, most prior haptic virtual keyboard devices were cumbersome, slow to response, and expensive.
This study creates a portable haptic virtual keyboard system for fully immersive VR environments based on our prior pilot work (Wu et al. 2012). The main purpose of this study is to help users better interact with a keyboard in a fully immersive VR environment, without taking off the HMDs. If a user needs a keyboard in a fully immersive VR environment, most cases are to change some data or parameters, not for conventional long documentary typing task. Therefore, in this research, the virtual keyboard is developed mainly for typing short commands in a fully immersive VR environment.
There are two research questions in this study. The ﬁrst question is that does a virtual keyboard perform better than

a physical keyboard in a fully immersive VR environment? The second question is that does a virtual keyboard with haptic feedback perform better than the one without haptic feedback? The organization of the paper is as follows. Section 2 describes different keyboards, the measurement of a physical keystroke, and the method for creating realistic simulated haptic feedback. Section 3 provides the system implementation. Section 4 describes the user test and test results. Section 5 offers conclusions and discussions.
2 Haptic feedback of realistic keystroke
2.1 Different keyboards
Based on different mechanisms, keyboards can be classiﬁed into four kinds: mechanical, membrane, conductive rubber, and capacitive keyboards. The most popular ones in the market are membrane keyboards and mechanical keyboards. Membrane keyboards use rubber domes as switch design, and when a key is pressed, the corresponding rubber dome will buckle, so the front layer and back layer of the membrane connect to close a circuit. Mechanical keyboards use springs and stems as switch design, and when a key is pressed, the stem pushes the spring down, so the two metal contacts connect. In this study, a standard membrane laptop keyboard with 1.5-mm keystroke was used to measure the haptic feedback.
2.2 Keystroke measurement
To create realistic simulated keyboard haptic feedback, real haptic feedback from a physical keyboard needs to be measured ﬁrst. In this study, a key press device with a high torque servo motor was used to stably press and release a physical key from a physical keyboard. A non-contact laser interferometer (Polytec OFV-502) was used to measure the displacement and velocity of a key press. A pressure sensor (FSR402) was used to measure the force on a key. Figure 1 shows the key press device and a pressure sensor placed on top of a key.
Prior studies show that the ‘‘clicks’’ that users feel when they press and release a key are caused by buckling and restitution forces (Tashiro et al. 2009). The buckling and restitution forces are caused by the rubber dome that is used to support the key. When the key is pressed, the rubber dome buckles at the buckling force. When the key is released, the rubber dome is restored at the restitution force.
The acceleration data of both buckling and restitution were found by taking the ﬁrst derivative of the measured velocity of the keystroke. Figure 2 shows the measured

