The MATLAB neural network toolbox was used to train the neural network. The average root–mean-square training error was calculated automatically by comparing the neural network output samples to the known input signals. The average root-mean-square training error for all 300 training signals was 4.91 %. The results show that the neural network was well trained. Figure 5 shows one input signal and the corresponding neural network output samples.
After the transfer function was modeled, the acceleration data of the keystroke were used as the input signal for the neural network. Then, the neural network created the input signals for the micro-speakers. Therefore, the microspeakers can create vibrations that matched the vibrations of the keystroke.
Figure 6 shows a micro-speaker used in this study. Inside a micro-speaker, an electromagnet is placed in front of a permanent magnet. The permanent magnet is ﬁxed, but the electromagnet is movable. Electricity pulses pass through the coil of the electromagnet to change its magnetic direction, which will in turn attract and repel the permanent magnet, causing vibration.
Figure 7 shows the original acceleration from a real key and the simulated acceleration from a micro-speaker. The acceleration can be divided into two phases: buckling phase and restitution phase. According to Bensma¨ıa et al. (2005), human perception of frequency vibrations relies on the spectral content of the signals. In other words, two vibrations might have different waveforms, but they might cause similar tactile perception if their spectral content of the signals is similar. Therefore, spectrum analysis was used to compare the vibrotactile effects. By using Fourier Transform, the frequency spectrums of the two phases can be obtained. Figure 8 shows the normalized frequency spectrums of the two phases of the original vibration and the simulated vibration. The peaks of the frequency

