Condition ‘‘P’’ had a mean completion time of 41.4 s (r = 10.9). Condition ‘‘V’’ had a mean completions time of 99.4 s (r = 36.3). Condition ‘‘VH’’ had a mean completion time of 82.4 s (r = 37.5). Even if the participants needed to take off the HMD to use a physical keyboard, participants spent the least time to complete the task. It might be because some of the participants input the correct information using the physical keyboard even without looking at the keys. On the other hand, when the participants used the virtual keyboard, it took time for them to locate the virtual hands on top of the right key.
Furthermore, one-way ANOVA was conducted to analyze the differences among the three test conditions. The p value of the one-way ANOVA for all questions is shown in Table 3. The bold value in Table 3 means that there is no signiﬁcant difference among the three test conditions. In this study, real-time performance means that when users press a key, they can immediately receive haptic feedback. Results show that except the comparison of real-time, all other questions show signiﬁcant differences in at least one comparison. Thus, a post hoc analysis was conducted.
The p value of the Scheffe post hoc test for all questions is shown in Table 4. Table 4 shows that there is no signiﬁcant difference in real-time performance for the three conditions, indicating that both VR keyboards offer similar real-time performance compared to the physical keyboard. However, the average score of condition VH is slightly higher than

condition P. It might be because participants needed to take off the HMD when they used the physical keyboard. As a result, they might feel inconvenient and thus rated the ‘‘realtime’’ performance of the physical keyboard lower.
Concerning comfort, intuitiveness, convenience, smoothness, and willingness to use again, both VR keyboards with and without haptic feedback all show signiﬁcant differences compared with the physical keyboard. The results show that participants preferred to use the VR keyboard in a fully immersive virtual environment because they did not need to take off the HMD to use a physical key board. Therefore, the sensation of immersion and realism was not interrupted. The results answered the ﬁrst research question that a virtual keyboard performs better than a physical keyboard in a fully immersive VR environment.
Concerning comfort, intuitiveness, convenience, smoothness, and willingness to use again, there is no signiﬁcant difference between the virtual keyboard with and without haptic feedback. In addition, a t test was conducted to compare the realism between conditions ‘‘V’’ and ‘‘VH.’’ The results show that there is signiﬁcant difference between virtual keyboard with and without haptic feedback (p = .035). It indicates that the virtual keyboard with haptic feedback offers better realistic effect. In addition, participants spent 16.7 % less time on condition ‘‘VH’’ than condition ‘‘V.’’ It indicates that the addition of a haptic interface increases the efﬁciency of the system. Haptic feedback gave participants more conﬁdence in successfully pressing a correct key in a fully immersive VR

